The bad code uses `HttpServletResponse`'s `setHeader` to set a header with a value that contains a user-supplied input, which an attacker could craft to make the header name match a real header name (e.g., `X-Forwarded-Proto`), allowing the attacker to override the server's own header and inject arbitrary headers into the response. This is a classic header injection vulnerability.

```java
// Bad: setting a header with user input, vulnerable to header injection
HttpServletResponse response = ...;
response.setHeader("X-Forwarded-Proto", "http://evil.com");
response.setHeader("X-Real-IP", "127.0.0.1");
```

***

The good code uses `HttpServletResponse`'s `setHeader` only for server-defined headers and avoids setting any header with user input. The `X-Forwarded-Proto` header is set by the client, so it should never be set by the server.

```java
// Good: server-defined headers only, no user input in header names
HttpServletResponse response = ...;
response.setHeader("Server", "MyServer");
response.setHeader("X-Real-IP", "127.0.0.1"); // client-provided, server shouldn't set it
```

